## 13. RESPONSIVE DESIGN FOR EVERY DEVICE

The entire website must work on:

- Small mobile phones.
- Large mobile phones.
- Tablets.
- Small laptops.
- Standard laptops.
- Desktop monitors.
- Large desktop displays.
- High-resolution screens.

Do not treat responsive design as a final cosmetic adjustment.

Design every page with mobile usability in mind from the beginning.

### Mobile requirements

On small screens:

- Navigation must work using touch.
- Menus must open and close reliably.
- Text must remain readable.
- Hero headlines must wrap correctly.
- Buttons must be easy to tap.
- Pricing cards must fit the viewport.
- Pricing comparisons must remain usable.
- Forms must have comfortable input sizes.
- Images must scale without distortion.
- Cards must not become excessively narrow.
- Legal documents must remain readable.
- Animations must not cause horizontal overflow.
- Sticky elements must not cover important content.
- Contact controls must not obscure the page.
- Footer columns must stack cleanly.

Never solve mobile layout problems by shrinking all content until it fits.

Reorganise the layout appropriately.

### Breakpoint strategy

Use content-driven responsive breakpoints.

A reasonable starting point is:

- Small phones: 320–374 px.
- Standard phones: 375–479 px.
- Large phones: 480–767 px.
- Tablets: 768–1023 px.
- Laptops: 1024–1439 px.
- Large desktops: 1440 px and above.

These are guidelines, not rigid limits.

Use fluid sizing through CSS Grid, Flexbox, clamp(), minmax(), and responsive image sizing where appropriate.

Test at widths of 320, 360, 375, 390, 430, 768, 1024, 1280, 1440, and 1920 pixels.

There must be no unintended horizontal scrolling.

